  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe will develop a new approach based on Fourier analysis and optimization of the overlap integral. The key idea is that the optimal solution can be expressed as a function with low Fourier coefficients. We will:\n   - Represent the step function as a truncated Fourier series\n   - Optimize the Fourier coefficients to minimize the maximum overlap\n   - Combine this with a constrained optimization using CVXPY to ensure feasibility\n   - This approach is novel because it directly uses the spectral properties of the function to capture its global structure, addressing the non-convex nature of the problem\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Representation: Use a Fourier series of the form h(x) = a0/2 + sum_{k=1}^m [ak cos(2\u03c0 k x / \u03bb) + bk sin(2\u03c0 k x / \u03bb)] with periodicity \u03bb=2 (the domain length)\n- Constraints: \n  0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x (handled via projection after optimization)\n  \u222b_0^2 h(x) dx = 1 (normalized)\n- Objective: Minimize C = max_{k} \u222b_{-\u221e}^{\u221e} h(x) (1-h(x+k)) dx (full correlation over the entire domain)\n- Formulation: CVXPY will be used to optimize the coefficients with constraints on Fourier coefficients that guarantee 0\u2264h\u22641\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na) Validate parent and create initial_h_values if needed\nb) Generate candidate by:\n  - Projecting initial parent to low-resolution grid if necessary\n  - Selecting optimal Fourier series order m based on grid size\n  - Optimizing coefficients using CVXPY with:\n    \u2022 Fourier series truncation\n    \u2022 Fourier coefficient constraints that enforce 0\u2264h\u22641\n    \u2022 Bounded optimization within feasible domain\nc) Optimization steps:\n  1. Fourier-based initialization using the parent function\n  2. Local refinement with coordinate descent\n  3. Global refinement with adaptive random shifts\n  4. Final smoothing using gradient projection\nd) Testing and validation:\n  \u2022 Verify 0\u2264h\u22641 and correct area using projection\n  \u2022 Compute overlap via exact integral or Python convolution\n  \u2022 Maintain grid resolution relationship (e.g., n_point = 2m for Fourier series order)\n\n4. Search choices and auto-tuning\n   \u2022 Fourier order: m = n_points // 6 (range 10-20)\n   \u2022 Optimization phase triggers:\n     - Use phase 1 if C > 0.3\n     - Use phase 2 if C > 0.35\n     - Use phase 3 if C > 0.38\n   \u2022 Adaptive random shifts: exponentially-decaying shift size (between -1 and 1)\n   \u2022 Feasibility repair: after each evaluation, project to [0,1] with area preservation\n\n5. Computation budget\n   - Phase 1: 10% - Lightweight Fourier optimization\n   - Phase 2: 30% - Heavy coordinate descent with random shifts\n   - Phase 3: 60% - Global search with grid increases and restarts\n   - Termination depth: 3 refinement levels\n   - Fallback: Best candidate at grid size n=500\n\n6. Validation and outputs\n   - Feasibility checks: 0\u2264h\u22641 and sum(h)=n/2\n   - Objective validation: recomputation using both Fourier and direct convolution\n   - Termination triggers:\n     \u2022 Best improvement < 1e-4 in 100 steps\n     \u2022 Over time budget limit\n   - Output format: h_values, C5_bound, n_points",
